    "problem": "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{\u2033}-x{y}^{\u2032}+y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{\u2033}-x{y}^{\u2032}+y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(1)=4,\\,x>0.\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=4.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{\u2032} & ={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{\u2032}(1)=4$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =1+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=x+3x\\operatorname{ln}\u2061x.\n$$",
